course: dictionary
lesson: 9450
title: Standard: the usual, or the agreed way
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Most goods now cross the sea in standard steel boxes on giant ships.» Here standard means the usual, ordinary kind: boxes that are all the same shape and size.
explain: «Standard» can also be a noun, meaning a level or a rule. Teachers keep «high standards» for work, which means how good the work should be. In «the standard form is "I was going to the shop"», standard means the agreed version that is used in school writing and tests.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Kings and cities set standard weights and measures so buyers were not cheated.» Does "agreed, usual measure that everyone uses" fit? Yes, because a standard weight must be the same for everyone, so nobody is tricked.
fact: Standard (adjective) means normal, usual, or the ordinary kind.
fact: A standard (noun) can be a level of quality, a rule for behaviour, an agreed measurement, or a flag on a pole.
fact: «High standards» means work or behaviour that is very good, and «meet the standard» means reaching the level that is expected.
fact: Standard units, like metres and kilograms, let everyone measure the same way.
fact: Standard English is the agreed form used in school writing and tests, while dialect has its own rules, as in «I were going to't shop».
exercise: «Most goods now cross the sea in standard steel boxes on giant ships.» In this sentence, what does standard mean? | Made of wood, painted red | *The usual, ordinary kind | Hidden under the water | Very expensive and rare
exercise: Miss Patel says, «Our class must meet the standard for neat handwriting.» What is a standard here? | *A level of quality that work should reach | A list of the shortest words | A flag carried on a pole | A kind of metal box
exercise: «I seen it» is common in speech, but what is the standard form for writing? | I sawed it | I seen it | *I saw it | I seed it
test: The metre is a standard unit. What does standard mean in this phrase? | A flag on a boat | *An agreed measure that everyone uses | A very long piece of wood | A rule about how to behave
test: «Kings and cities set standard weights and measures so buyers were not cheated.» Why did they need standard weights? | So buyers would get less | So the weights could be stolen | *So everyone measured the same way and nobody was tricked | So the weights would look pretty
exam: Which sentence uses standard as an adjective meaning normal? | Teachers keep high standards for work | *The shop sells a standard size of bread tin | Standards of behaviour are set by the school | The soldiers carried a standard on a tall pole
